1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,806
These temple walls are written in Tamil.

2
00:00:02,806 --> 00:00:04,505
The letters are fading.

3
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:09,628
From Madurai to Thanjavur, kings
carved their words in stone.

4
00:00:09,628 --> 00:00:13,524
Thanjavur's great temple has
stood over a thousand years.

5
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:18,795
Madurai itself is carved into
them: twenty-five editions name it.

6
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:22,133
Time wears the letters away.

7
00:00:22,133 --> 00:00:27,843
65% of the 1,320 inscriptions
we studied are damaged.

8
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:35,216
In 2021, India had about 23 epigraphists
for nearly one lakh inscriptions.

9
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:38,395
And training a new reader is slow.

10
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:42,660
So I built an apprentice.

11
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:45,382
Meet Ezhuthu: in Tamil, 'letter'.

12
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:50,251
On a real Chola-period
stone from Thanjavur,

13
00:00:50,251 --> 00:00:54,582
it reads forty-six carved letters,
each with its own confidence,

14
00:00:54,582 --> 00:00:58,751
then the words, a phrase in
today's Tamil, and its meaning.

15
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:05,159
Practice turns real
inscriptions into 1,469 drills:

16
00:01:05,159 --> 00:01:08,268
letters, words, gaps and formulas.

17
00:01:08,268 --> 00:01:09,568
Tamil first.

18
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:12,882
Restore proposes the worn-away letters.

19
00:01:12,882 --> 00:01:16,914
In our recorded gallery run,
it filled four gaps of ten;

20
00:01:16,914 --> 00:01:19,707
all four match the editors' restoration.

21
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:23,905
Where it is unsure, it
leaves the gap open.

22
00:01:23,905 --> 00:01:28,954
And that doubt becomes the lesson:
experts are unsure here too.

23
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:35,089
Six agents, built with
Strands Agents, pass the stone along.

24
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:38,693
The Reader turns photos into letters.

25
00:01:38,693 --> 00:01:42,194
The Restorer checks
parallels and a 5-gram model,

26
00:01:42,194 --> 00:01:47,666
then asks Claude on Amazon Bedrock:
Sonnet first, Opus when it matters.

27
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:53,421
Then a calibrated confidence check
decides: fill it, or leave it open.

28
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:56,736
The Interpreter turns
letters into meaning.

29
00:01:56,736 --> 00:02:02,128
A Strands Graph runs every lesson, with
a Swarm of Tutor and Assessor inside.

30
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:09,624
Agents look up kings, temples and formulas
in a custom Tamil ontology over MCP.

31
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:13,465
We fine-tuned Strands decision
models for Tamil:

32
00:02:13,465 --> 00:02:18,229
train stones only, a pre-registered
pick, one held-out test.

33
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:24,457
The live app runs on Amazon Bedrock
AgentCore, serverless, as code.

34
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:27,567
Students practise on real stones.

35
00:02:27,567 --> 00:02:30,662
Temple visitors point
a phone at Tamil text.

36
00:02:30,662 --> 00:02:34,764
And what it cannot settle goes
to an epigraphist's review queue.

37
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:44,515
On 413 held-out test gaps, 62.7%
match the editors' restoration.

38
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:48,389
When it commits, nine in ten fills match.

39
00:02:48,389 --> 00:02:50,897
When it doesn't know, it says so.

40
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:55,097
Ezhuthu. Learn to read the stone.

41
00:02:55,097 --> 00:02:56,877
Help read the backlog.
